've  appointed more than 150 nominees who  have been confirmed who are not straight  white men you had an opportunity with  this nomination to for the first time in  the nation's history if the Justice was  can  to have a Supreme Court that was not a  majority of straight white men why  didn't you take that opportunity well  because I never think about it in terms  of this seat is for a Hispanic man this  seat is for a gay black woman that he  you know that's not how I think about  those issues what I think is that we  should nominate people who are extremely  well-qualified who have shown themselves  to care about people and care about  justice and when you cast a wide net and  you look at all the people who are  qualified then you're going to end up  with a court that reflects America and  that's what we've done with our District  Court nominees that's what we've done  with our appellate court nominees as you  point out I've appointed more minorities  more women more LGBTQ judges than any in  history but I've done so taking each  appointment and saying who's the best  person for the job at this time and it's  just turned out that by doing so and by  looking at a wide range of experience  not just the traditional they were at a  law school and they were a prosecutor  but also people who were public  defenders or people who were advocates  on behalf of you know workers or what  have you that you do get a broad pool of  candidates in this particular situation  what I was looking for was the best  person for the Supreme Court at this  time and somebody who could bring both  conservatives and liberals together  because we don't want the court being as  polarized as our politics and Judge  Garland was the perfect candidate for  this moment for this particular seat as  you know a previous that I nominated two  women one of them Hispanic tips out of  my aura  all right judge Kagan and Justice Kagan  and they're just doing a great job just  this past week the court split for four  on a decision whether or not to allow an  execution to go forward in Alabama that  there were four justices a lot of people  said if Justice Scalia had still been on  the Portland voting that the execution  probably would have gone forward there  there were some liberal lawyers  including David Mitchell who said that  they had the same concern about judge  garland given his prosecutor past which  you've obviously touted in his  nomination he said he'd rather have an  eight-member court what do you say to  people who have that concern about this  nomination here's the thing Chris what  you don't want to do is to start  thinking about the courts in terms of  how will they come out on this  particular case because judges look at  all kinds of cases and you know judge  Garland's background and experience  means that he cares about public safety  he and there are times where he is ruled  in favor of law enforcement and making  sure that they had the tools they need  to keep us safe there are also times  where he has pushed back against what he  considered to be law enforcement  overreach including by the federal  government and by the president the  United States and his administration so  what you really are looking for is just  a judge who is going to play it straight  and you know apply the law not try to  solve every ill in society that's not  the role of the courts not try to engage  in all kinds of radical steps but rather  just to take the law in the Constitution  and say how does it apply to today with  an understanding that there are  real-life consequences to his or her  decision and I hope one of the things I  look for in my nominees is that they  have some sense of  passion and empathy they're not just  always going to be looking out for the  most powerful in society but there are  going to be thinking about ordinary  people and how the laws impacting them  in a practical way you talk about the  important issues that the judges are  going to notice in deciding obviously in  recent weeks there's been a lot of  attention on transgender people and  obviously your Attorney General spoke  very forcefully about the lawsuit that  the administration is brought against  North Carolina you followed up with  guidance that came from the Education  Department for all schools um there were  governors who responded to that by by  saying that that local official should  disregard that guidance what what do you  have to say to to those governors well  first of all on the on the merits on the  issue itself we're talking about kids  and anybody who has been in school and  in high school who's been a parent I  think should realize that kids who are  sometimes in the minority kids who you  know have a different sexual orientation  or transgender are subject to a lot of  bullying potentially there's they are  vulnerable and I think that is part of  our obligation as a society to make sure  that everybody is treated fairly and our  kids are all loved and that they're  protected and that their dignity is  affirmed and  in this particular situation I won't  comment on the lawsuits that are going  back and forth between the Justice  Department and North Carolina that's  typically not my role I try to with the  Justice Department do its job but with  respect to the department education  schools were asking us we're asking the  Department of Education how should we  handle this so they they they sought out  some clarification some guidance and  that's what we did we said you know what  it is our view that you should try to  treat these kids with dignity here's how  there are school districts who have been  wrestling with this problem and have we  think done a good job in accommodating  them in a way that is good for everybody  and so you can learn from these best  practices this is what we are advising  and you know ultimately depending on how  these other lawsuits go you know courts  will affirm or reject how we see the  issue but we think it was important for  schools who want to go ahead and in a  very practical way try to deal with the  school year what are they going to be  doing next year how should we approach  this that we give them our best judgment  about how to approach it one last  question  we've got our Facebook live audience  here YouTube audience you've obviously  seen a lot with the courts in the  judiciary in your time in office that  has probably changed your views since  you were a law professor since you were  doing that there's been this this effort  from from states from individuals from  the house to to take action against you  and go to the courts against your your  administrative actions do you do you  think that this is something that's  unique to your presidency do you think  that this is part of a in emerging  pattern of how states and others are  going to be dealing with the government  no I mean I look there's a long history  of this if you think about we're in the  Roosevelt Room right now there was a guy  named Franklin Delano Roosevelt his  pictures right over your shoulder who  was dealing with the Great Depression  and was trying to initiate a whole range  of actions to to try to help lift people  out of poverty and give them jobs and  you had a cantankerous court that was  striking down a lot of the rules that he  put forward you know this is part of the  process of our democracy for the most  part even Bush appointed judges have  upheld what I've done on things like  health care and on trying to combat  climate change and trying to make sure  that our justice system is fair  occasionally they disagree and and  that's okay you know it's part of the  process the main thing I want viewers to  understand is that they're really big  stakes to this right now for example we  just had a case that was deadlocked for  four that had to do with whether or not  a married woman had to get a signature  from her husband to get a loan from a  bank to start a business and the  question was whether the Federal Reserve  Bank which oversees our a lot of our  banking system and monetary system along  with some of the other agencies have the  authority to tell banks you know that's  discriminatory you can't treat women  that way you're not treating a man  that way when he's going in for a loan  and because we didn't have a knife judge  justice on the Supreme Court right now  in Arkansas the rule is different than  it is in Tennessee and that's not how  you you want the United States of  America operate states have their own  powers and they can make some of their  own laws but ultimately we also have one  United States law on certain big issues  and the Supreme Court has to be there to  deal with it and what I really want  everybody to understand is that the way  that we keep our judicial system intact  and with the integrity that it has had  and that is admired around the world the  way we maintain rule of law and a sense  that justice is blind and it doesn't  favor one group or another but is  properly interpreting the laws that  Congress passes and the actions that the  president is taking and the language of  the Constitution the way to preserve  that is to make sure that when we  nominate judges they're treated fairly  that we try to minimize the politics and  that well-qualified people can take  those seats and be outside of the  day-to-day scrum of politics and  I think if we're going to maintain that  tradition of judicial integrity and the  integrity of the Supreme Court then I'm  going to need people who are watching to  put pressure on your Senators to say do  your job and just be fair if you're fair  if you're doing your job then I have  confidence that the people I've  nominated or highly qualified and that  they'll continue the tradition of a  judiciary that is not subject to the  same day-to-day polling and politics and  you know nonsense that we see on TV  every day but it's standing above it and  taking the long view it and treating our  democracy a little bit more seriously  that's what we want I know it just  happened today but do you have any  response to the courts action on the  Obamacare contraception mandate well  they basically punted it the circuit the  practical effect is right now that women  will still continue to be able to get  contraception if they are getting health  insurance and we are properly  accommodating religious institutions who  have objections to contraception you  know I I won't speculate as to why they  punted but my suspicion is is if we had  nine Supreme Court justices or might  have been a different outcome  all right thanks Chris Fritz Kuhns thank  you so much majority yeah all right  thank you guys thank you Facebook in  YouTube oh good
 

